WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.650
Welcome to Academy Dialogues.

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.360
My name is Shawn Finnie.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.030
I am honored to guide
this conversation today

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.880
with three individuals
that I believe

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.310
are shaping our
culture through film,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:15.330
and that is writer-director Gina
Prince-Bythewood, Oscar winner

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:20.310
TJ Martin, and the incomparable,
incredible Delroy Lindo.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.030
Welcome, everybody.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:22.830
Thank you.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:23.350
Thank you.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.610
First, I mean, when we say
it's good to see you now,

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.740
it just takes on a
whole new level based

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.413
on where we are in the world.

00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:31.830
So first, I want
to just check in.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.743
How are you doing?

00:00:33.743 --> 00:00:34.910
Gina, we can start with you.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:35.750
How are you doing?

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.030
How are you managing
in this time?

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:39.950
It's so funny because
that's my go to is

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.320
to you say, how are you?

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.710
And it's such a loaded question.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:52.800
Yeah, it's a very
scary time right now.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.250
A couple weeks ago, I was saying
that it felt like a hopeful

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:02.430
time, given that it felt that
true change was finally coming,

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.430
that in this moment, instead
of people being defensive,

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.860
people were finally
understanding

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.570
what we've been fighting for
all these years and hearing us.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:17.340
But right now, I've gone back
to it feeling like a scary time.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.680
And I think what's
indicative is my son, who's

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.840
19, get pulled over by
police for the first time.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:29.430
When?

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:30.570
When, Gina?

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.200
When?

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.430
When?

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.130
Yeah, when was your
son pulled over?

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.600
Two days ago.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.340
Wow.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.560
And the fact that my
husband I didn't even

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.560
have the luxury of being
able to be angry at him

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.000
for getting a speeding ticket.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.420
Our first question
was are you OK?

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.490
And then, he survived it.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:57.000
And the fact that that's where
our mind goes is pretty insane.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.050
Wow.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:02.280
And Delroy, I know you also
have a son, so I'm sure when--

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.130
19, I have a 19-year old, yeah.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:08.370
So when you hear that as a
creative, but also as a human

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:12.830
and as a parent, how does
that register with you?

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:13.967
What Gina just said?

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:15.050
Yeah, what Gina just said.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.170
First of all, the fact
that Gina acknowledged

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.440
that it is a loaded question--

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.610
and it is.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.580
And it's loaded
from my standpoint

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:35.600
because it is chock full of
so many potential responses

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.320
that one could have to the
question, how are you doing?

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:45.020
One can respond in any number
of different directions.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.290
When I hear Gina saying that
her 19-year-old was pulled over,

00:02:53.290 --> 00:03:03.310
and my 19-year-old has been
pulled over and was also--

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:09.370
he wrecked his car a few
months ago in a car wreck.

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:11.410
Well, no, he wrecked his car.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.720
He's fine.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:21.770
But I really relate to the
various responses that one has.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.150
Are you OK?

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.480
It happened at 3:00
in the morning.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.100
My lady ended up
going to where he was.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.500
Thank god somebody
had stopped, a brother

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:43.010
had stopped and stayed with
him until my lady and a law

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.680
enforcement officer got there.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.540
It happened that
the law enforcement

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.740
officer was reasonable,
a reasonable human being.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.990
So all of that was positive.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:05.600
But you know what?

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:15.430
I think the most honest
or authentic thing

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:18.430
that I can say in terms of
responding to that question

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:19.555
is that there is a--

00:04:23.289 --> 00:04:28.800
I don't want to say a
deadening of responses,

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.630
of emotional
responses, but there

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:35.780
is a blunting of responses,
just because there are

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:38.045
so many responses that one has.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:49.210
To your question, how am I
doing, in context of everything

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:54.070
that's going on, I'm doing
OK and probably-- no,

00:04:54.070 --> 00:05:02.020
not probably, better than OK
because I'm working right now.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:05.560
Millions of people
are not, as we know.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:11.480
I am able to, therefore, because
I'm working, support my family.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:19.210
That is an extraordinary,
almost a luxury in these times.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:23.110
So I am constantly
attempting to take a half

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:32.850
a step back from my present
and try at least to figure out

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.290
perspective.

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:40.020
So there are these multi
steps that one takes.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.210
And so--

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.560
That's the most honest, and
thank you for the honesty.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.010
And I feel like this
Academy Dialogues,

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:51.680
this whole thing was created
to give our members a platform

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.840
to speak their truth
and to talk about all

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.570
of the issues and things that
are happening in the community

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:58.670
as it relates to art
and as it just relates

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:00.140
to the human experience.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:01.430
TJ, how are you--

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:03.800
I would like to add one more
thing before you go to TJ.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.190
And not to dwell on
this, and perhaps

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:10.610
we can discuss it later
in the conversation

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:12.530
if it comes back
around, but when

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:17.390
Gina said a few weeks ago
she was feeling hopeful,

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:20.840
my response was why?

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:27.570
And I respond that
way not as a challenge

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:32.400
to Gina or anybody else,
but I'm curious about--

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:36.240
I find myself being curious
about what are the components?

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.500
What were the components
of your hopefulness

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.980
weeks or months
ago, whenever it was

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:44.790
it you were feeling hopeful?

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:46.860
And what are the
components that have

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:51.700
caused your current response?

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:55.570
Because that's a whole other
series of conversations.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:07:00.550
But again, I just
wanted to mention that.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:02.620
We can definitely
go to that right

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.120
after this, right after TJ.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.930
Yeah, yeah.

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:08.670
TJ, how are you?

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:14.800
As everyone pointed out, it's
a weighted question, right?

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:23.080
If I'm going to be completely
honest, I'm processing, right?

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:27.380
In active processing, I
think there's a wide range

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.060
of emotions that come with it.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:34.920
I think for me,
in terms of like--

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.920
I have a tremendous amount
of anxiety right now.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.170
Part of that probably is in
a really practical manner.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:41.690
I'm in between
projects, so I tend

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:47.690
to process through the
work, and it brings my mind

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.200
at ease to be able to
actually take that energy,

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:55.790
and package it, and
put it in a direction.

00:07:55.790 --> 00:08:02.170
And without that,
the ups and downs

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:08.520
are very visceral
and palpable for me.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.665
And I don't think it's
because of necessarily that--

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:16.820
it's not because things
have fundamentally

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:19.050
changed by any means.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:24.890
It's that the flaws of this
country, just the window,

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:27.212
the curtains have been opened.

00:08:27.212 --> 00:08:28.295
And the curtains are open.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:37.120
I'm appalled by the
inability to actually

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.130
have an empathetic
conversation about who

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:44.200
we are as a community.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:48.820
And that, I find
really unnerving.

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:56.000
Even with this kind of really
diverse coalition of allies

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.720
and what allyship
looks like in 2020,

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:03.615
the blowback is
equally discouraging.

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:11.490
And I find that we're
all processing out loud

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:13.320
on our social media feeds.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.060
We're all processing internally.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:21.570
And I don't necessarily think
we're having healthy dialogue.

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:23.580
I think this is why this is
so important because I think

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:25.320
it is one of the few forums
where you can actually

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:26.320
have a healthy dialogue.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:35.220
To Delroy's point, there's
not one dominant emotion.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.710
It's minute-to-minute,
day-by-day, hour-by-hour,

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:39.630
at least for me.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.310
I had a ton of
anxiety this morning.

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:43.920
And then by noon, I spoke.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:49.350
I did a webinar with a bunch
of young filmmakers at USC,

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:54.300
specifically Black women,
that are doing projects,

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.850
nonfiction projects as a result
of the current civil unrest.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.040
A lot of products are
motivated by that,

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:04.260
and that brought
me a ton of ease.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:05.790
And then, now, I feel good.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:08.430
And then by tonight, maybe
I'll be tripping again.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:14.800
It's an ongoing dialogue with
myself and the world, right?

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.540
I mean, the most honest way you
said that is processing, right?

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:19.290
Which means you're
neither here nor there.

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:20.480
You're in transition.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.070
And going back to what
Delroy mentioned earlier,

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:25.800
we talk a lot about change,
the need for change, change,

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:26.300
change.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:27.717
But in order for
things to change,

00:10:27.717 --> 00:10:30.450
something has to have changed.

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:33.570
And so to your point, Delroy,
when you were asking Gina,

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:36.030
and you were saying you
were feeling hopeful before,

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:37.790
what gave you that hope, Gina?

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:41.490
What brought you to that point,
to Delroy's question earlier?

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:44.250
I feel that it was
a couple things.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:46.920
I mean, it was certainly,
as TJ just mentioned,

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.590
the allyship felt
different this time.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.160
It didn't feel simplistic.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.900
It felt that there were true
allies coming out and putting

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:04.230
themselves in harm's
way in the way

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:09.240
that we've had to by
ourselves within the--

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:15.300
Kamala being chosen
was a big deal.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:19.080
Watching the
Democratic Convention

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:23.220
made me feel hope
about what could be.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:26.280
And then within our
industry, to finally

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:31.550
have conversations with
people that I've worked with

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:36.370
and had those hard
conversations.

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:41.320
One in particular-- why do I
need to walk into your offices

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:46.720
and not pass one Black person
from the reception desk

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:49.600
all the way to
your corner office?

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.350
And for the first time
people not being defensive,

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:57.310
but actually accepting that they
have messed up in a big way,

00:11:57.310 --> 00:12:04.030
and having a certainty about
changing it, and knowing

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:05.365
why it needed to be changed.

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:10.390
And then the other
thing was there

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:14.800
are so many collectives
of Black artists

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:18.640
coming together from
all of it, from acting,

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:20.830
directing, producing,
below the line.

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:23.530
We're all coming together
in such a big way

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:26.890
for the first time and
seeing and understanding

00:12:26.890 --> 00:12:27.950
and feeling our power.

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:31.360
And that has felt really
good because we've never

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:34.670
done that before.

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:39.350
But I think the thing
that changed for me--

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:44.500
and it's been a
slow grind back--

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:49.510
this kid, this 17-year-old
kid who killed the protesters,

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:52.850
and the fact that
he is being excused

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:57.790
by the White House and others.

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:02.530
I cannot wrap my head around how
far we have fallen as a country

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:03.650
when that is happening.

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:08.200
And from that point on, it
does seem, as TJ mentioned,

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.870
that we've got this
great allyship.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:14.410
But the blowback is starting
to feel even stronger.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:16.030
And I just know
the fight that we

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:21.700
are in in this moment,
literally for survival.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:24.750
When you're talking about
the community aspect,

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:27.220
I resonated because in
our community, like you

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:30.250
said before, maybe we weren't
as collaborative before

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:32.800
or weren't working
together as closely.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:36.730
And for all of you, are you
noticing that change as we

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:38.170
go to the younger generation?

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:41.050
Are you noticing that they're
wanting to work together more?

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:43.420
And what can we learn
from that, if so?

00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:52.840
Well, I work in nonfiction,
and our crews are not as big.

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.970
And my creative
partner and I, we

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:57.370
do a lot of the
heavy lifting, like

00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:00.550
from shooting to
cutting to everything.

00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:04.390
But when we're working
the commercial space,

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:06.625
we're obviously bigger crews.

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:17.440
I still think there's--

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:25.040
no pun intended-- but there's an
old guard within the industry.

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:28.570
I think there's a tremendous
amount of nepotism.

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:33.670
And even for a
younger generation

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:42.590
that has a vision for how
they want to participate

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:44.420
in the industry, I
still think they're

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:52.240
met with some really
rigid blockades.

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:55.960
And I think like a
lot of people, right?

00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:59.800
That becomes an
opportunity to mold

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:03.368
how you want to
handle that and how

00:15:03.368 --> 00:15:04.660
you want to integrate yourself.

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:06.010
And then once you
integrate yourself,

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:07.760
what do you do once
you integrate yourself

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.970
within the confines
of the industry,

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:16.490
even though it's a
day-to-day organized chaos.

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:21.810
But I think the thing
that I am encouraged by

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:28.570
is, like any institution,
it's unfortunate,

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:31.120
but it's also just
the way it is.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:35.290
I find that there are younger
voices advocating for change

00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:40.330
and forcing the hand of the
institution, AKA the industry,

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:45.010
to examine itself and
help facilitate change.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.330
That's always been the case
within the institution.

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:49.240
It's always an outside force.

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:53.020
But I love the fact that at
least younger voices are not

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:54.930
dropping the ball
in that regard.

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:58.540
And that, I find
really encouraging,

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:02.140
and I want to collaborate
with those people.

00:16:02.140 --> 00:16:04.223
We talk about,
often, Nina Simone

00:16:04.223 --> 00:16:05.890
has this quote, how
can you be an artist

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:07.820
and not reflect
the times, right?

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:10.000
And we talk about
that responsibility.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:13.960
And I wanted to ask you all,
and Delroy, starting with you,

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:16.810
is it the artist's
responsibility alone,

00:16:16.810 --> 00:16:19.165
or is it a collaboration
with the system,

00:16:19.165 --> 00:16:23.350
with the industry, with
that it is in your opinion?

00:16:26.185 --> 00:16:27.310
What do I think about that?

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:33.250
I think that, obviously, it is
a very personal and intimate

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:35.860
choice that one
makes for oneself.

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:40.210
But my personal
response to the question

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:45.070
is that, yes, I do
feel a responsibility.

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:47.140
But I want to say that--

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:49.210
and this goes back to
something you said, Shawn,

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:52.030
at the beginning of
the conversation--

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:57.850
having to do with I think
I'm a human being first.

00:16:57.850 --> 00:16:58.450
Yes.

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:02.680
For any other definitions,
descriptives, definitions,

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:04.829
I'm a human being first.

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:08.900
And I want to say
first and foremost.

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:14.230
So I think I'm always
responding as a human being,

00:17:14.230 --> 00:17:17.569
albeit that at a
certain point, I

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:21.440
am filtering my responses
through the conduit

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:23.760
of my creative self.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:27.970
But going back to
when we were talking

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.430
about our kids,
our 19-year-olds.

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:35.120
I'm responding as a parent
period, period the end.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:38.600
That's it-- no more and no less.

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:42.950
So to answer your question,
personally, yes, I

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:44.490
feel a responsibility.

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:50.370
But I think I am not defining my
various responses as an artist.

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:53.030
I think I'm defining
my responses

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:56.130
as a human being on the planet.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:57.300
Does that make sense?

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:59.920
That makes all of the
sense of the world.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:03.500
TJ, I would say to you,
following up with that,

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:06.700
how does that impact the
way that you approach film?

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:09.490
We saw you won the Oscar
for Undefeated, which

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:14.500
follows a Memphis football team
having a over 100-year losing

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:15.400
streak.

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:20.290
And that power of
them seeing themselves

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:23.410
as that and then what happened
when you came into it,

00:18:23.410 --> 00:18:25.420
how did that affect
your approach to coming

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:28.050
into working with them?

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:30.581
How did it affect my
approach in terms of?

00:18:30.581 --> 00:18:33.680
In terms of you're working with
the power of imagery, right?

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:36.650
You're working with a
team who sees themselves

00:18:36.650 --> 00:18:40.370
as losers for about 100
years, and the decision

00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:42.720
to highlight that and
how that inversely

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:46.190
changed the power of how they
saw themselves because they

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:48.210
ended up winning after that.

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:50.472
Well, they did not
win the-- well,

00:18:50.472 --> 00:18:51.930
I'd be giving my
part of the movie.

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:53.720
But in the season
that we covered,

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:56.435
they still were in
a losing streak.

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:04.660
So the challenge
with Undefeated was

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:09.820
that when you do a
verite film, you're

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:14.410
trying to express to the
audience, at least the way

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:18.670
my filmmaking partner and I see
it, the emotional experience

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:19.420
that you have.

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:23.770
Because we embedded ourselves
with this high school football

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:28.150
team in North Memphis for
the kids the senior year

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:32.380
of the first group of students
that came in and reshaped

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:35.260
the program with this
gentleman named Bill Courtney,

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:36.100
who was the coach.

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:44.180
The way with which the
students see themselves,

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:45.430
we can't take credit for that.

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:49.750
That's very much Bill
and the volunteers

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:55.750
that created a program that
were committed to the students

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:59.950
and to the student athletes
and were not doing it

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:04.030
for selfish purposes,
but rather that the coach

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:05.690
had moved to North Memphis.

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:07.720
He moved his business
there, and as a result,

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:09.387
he wanted to give
back to the community,

00:20:09.387 --> 00:20:13.030
so he was a permanent
fixture of the community.

00:20:13.030 --> 00:20:15.790
So the film can't
take credit for that.

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:20.680
But our job was to
go in and create--

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:23.740
can we capture the essence of
what the relationships were?

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:26.020
So a lot of the
biggest challenge

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:28.690
with Undefeated
in particular was

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:36.630
you've got a North Memphis,
which is 99.9% Black,

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:40.090
and you've got a white
coach, volunteer coach,

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:42.010
coming in from East Memphis.

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:45.040
And one of the things that
attracted us to the story

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:47.380
to begin with was
one of the players

00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:50.020
was spending part his
time with the coaches

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:52.450
in East Memphis living
there throughout the week

00:20:52.450 --> 00:20:55.150
and then living with his
grandmother in North Memphis

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:56.660
during the weekends.

00:20:56.660 --> 00:21:01.120
And it was an opportunity to use
either this particular student

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:07.120
or the team's story to
explore race and class

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:10.810
and use this experience
as a reflection of how

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:14.410
we talk about race and
class in the country.

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:16.330
But then, the football
narrative itself

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:19.390
became so good
and so fascinating

00:21:19.390 --> 00:21:22.210
that that's still present,
but through the conduit

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:24.100
of a football story.

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:30.110
But the bigger challenge
was I naturally

00:21:30.110 --> 00:21:32.510
go into something
like that going like,

00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:35.640
this particular coach, what
if he's got ulterior motives?

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:40.850
Is he giving the hard
sell on his relationship

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:42.320
with the students?

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:46.550
And the challenge after
spending time with them

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:55.280
was that there is a fundamental,
very real, very healthy

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:59.090
relationship between the
coach and the players.

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:03.500
Now, our job is to make
sure that we recognize

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:06.500
the blind spots in the baggage
that an audience is going

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:10.980
to come with that says is
this a white knight story,

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:13.790
which is a 100%
a valid question.

00:22:13.790 --> 00:22:18.820
So how do we take that
and say, good analysis,

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:21.950
but if we take it a
step further and we

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:25.550
create themes that are a little
bit more about fatherhood,

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:27.390
you actually transcend that.

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:34.600
And that became a
challenge creatively,

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:40.930
but it was something that
was very necessary to get

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:47.650
a film for people to actually
look beyond race and class

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:53.010
and acknowledge it at the same
time, you know what I mean?

00:22:53.010 --> 00:22:55.030
Because you're not
supposed to read it.

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:58.870
But then, can you say, oh, wow,
this is a real relationship.

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:01.810
And as a result, if we can get
that baggage out of the way,

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:04.210
then you're actually
looking at a coming

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:06.610
of age film, these
young men that

00:23:06.610 --> 00:23:10.960
are transitioning from
end of high school

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:13.270
into the early
stages of manhood.

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:17.890
And that's really what the
film was about in a space

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:23.450
created by volunteers that
allowed them to be vulnerable,

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:24.700
and that was huge.

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:28.780
And this is slightly
tangential, but that

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:32.110
is one thing I find I'm
always attracted to,

00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:37.060
which is so rarely seen
is finding spaces for men,

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:40.120
and particularly men of color,
and particularly Black men

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:43.810
to show their vulnerabilities
and feel comfortable

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:44.450
doing that.

00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:47.230
So our job at that
point is to continue

00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:53.490
to express to the audience
what we experienced

00:23:53.490 --> 00:23:56.610
through the emotions of it and
through the storytelling of it,

00:23:56.610 --> 00:23:59.645
and then preserve the
integrity of the space

00:23:59.645 --> 00:24:01.770
and the vulnerability that
they gave us and allowed

00:24:01.770 --> 00:24:06.963
us to capture in the
making of that film.

00:24:06.963 --> 00:24:08.880
So I don't know if that
answers your question.

00:24:11.410 --> 00:24:15.450
Gina, we were talking that
idea of the power of imagery

00:24:15.450 --> 00:24:17.380
and this idea of self-identity.

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:19.230
And you've been open
about your childhood,

00:24:19.230 --> 00:24:20.605
and growing up in
your community,

00:24:20.605 --> 00:24:23.010
and really being the only
one or not really seeing

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:24.420
people who looked like you.

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:27.000
How did that affect the
way that you approach film?

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:36.060
I mean, it certainly
absolutely defines it,

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:42.330
knowing very early the
power of film and imagery.

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.180
Just in quick context--

00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:49.190
adopted by a Salvadoran
mother and an Irish father

00:24:49.190 --> 00:24:53.130
and raised and in,
really, an all-white area.

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:56.400
And so growing up, just
never seeing myself

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:58.380
reflected, not even
in my own family.

00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:02.180
And that could do a number on
your self-esteem, certainly.

00:25:02.180 --> 00:25:07.560
And thank god for sports,
which gave me the applause I

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:10.590
was so desperately craving.

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:16.240
But I remember distinctly
being 17 years old

00:25:16.240 --> 00:25:19.095
and going to see the movie--

00:25:19.095 --> 00:25:20.720
I forgot what movie
I was going to see,

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:23.670
but the trailer for She's
Gotta Have It came on.

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:26.980
And looking up there
at Nola Darling,

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:30.570
and am I like Nola, who she is?

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:34.320
No, but it was me
seeing someone who

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:38.280
looked like me up on that big
screen, and I was floored.

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:40.590
And I've never
forgotten that feeling.

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:44.710
And for me, from that
moment and knowing

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:47.200
that I wanted to tell
stories and get into film

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:50.650
and television that I wanted
to give an audience, especially

00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:53.440
a Black audience, that
feeling that I had.

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:59.000
And so for me, my work has been
a fight to center our stories

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:02.290
and tell stories in every
genre, but through our lens.

00:26:02.290 --> 00:26:06.810
And yeah, and I'm
the first audience,

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:08.670
so I'm writing
what I want to see.

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:12.510
And then my hope is then
that I can inspire and create

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:16.020
characters that we
can aspire to be.

00:26:16.020 --> 00:26:19.590
And from there, the
world can see us

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:23.490
in all these different ways and
see the breadth of our humanity

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:26.670
and be inspired by us as well.

00:26:26.670 --> 00:26:30.230
And when you spoke,
I've heard you

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:33.590
say that when you are
able to see yourself, then

00:26:33.590 --> 00:26:35.210
you're able to see yourself.

00:26:35.210 --> 00:26:37.730
And what that does and how it
can alter and change someone's

00:26:37.730 --> 00:26:39.400
entire life--

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:42.200
I mean, obviously, the
opportunity and versatility,

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:42.700
right?

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:44.658
So when we're talking
about telling our stories

00:26:44.658 --> 00:26:47.210
in Black cinema, while
also knowing that it

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:49.290
is for the larger audience.

00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:52.400
It can be a Black story
and be universal as well.

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:55.590
Delroy, you've been
such a cultural icon

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:59.810
in a lot of Spike Lee's early
films and a staple, a staple

00:26:59.810 --> 00:27:04.280
in the community in
what you mean to us.

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:06.200
And I've also seen
your versatility.

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:08.700
And just, there was a time, I
think, you weren't in filming.

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:11.600
You were just
performing in theater.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:14.570
Have you always been able
to do that intersection?

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:17.990
Do you see us there in
theater to draw you to that?

00:27:17.990 --> 00:27:20.360
Because you represent something
so magical in that space

00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:21.380
as well.

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:28.460
First of all, were I am more
21st century individual,

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:31.970
I would have gone
on the internet

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:37.670
and looked up TJ and
Gina, and I did not.

00:27:37.670 --> 00:27:42.290
Therefore, TJ, I did
not know that you--

00:27:42.290 --> 00:27:46.310
that the film Undefeated,
which really moved me,

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:47.570
when you first--

00:27:47.570 --> 00:27:50.720
I did not know that you were
involved with Undefeated.

00:27:50.720 --> 00:27:54.890
So I want to say not only
congratulations to you

00:27:54.890 --> 00:27:56.510
as an audience.

00:27:56.510 --> 00:28:00.020
But an interesting
thing happened

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:04.400
when you first mentioned that
you are involved in that film.

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:06.620
I got goosebumps.

00:28:06.620 --> 00:28:08.330
And the reason that
I got goosebumps

00:28:08.330 --> 00:28:14.810
was because the depth of the
extent to which that film moved

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:17.220
me for a whole
variety of reasons.

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:19.820
So to the extent that
I gave Gina props,

00:28:19.820 --> 00:28:22.700
I want to give you
props right now.

00:28:22.700 --> 00:28:25.490
And if I were more
on the ball, I

00:28:25.490 --> 00:28:28.950
would have looked it up before
we got on this interview,

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:32.270
and I would have led
with that, right?

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:33.080
That's one thing.

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:35.480
So props, man,
major props to you.

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:43.130
And Gina, when you were
speaking about your upbringing,

00:28:43.130 --> 00:28:52.200
there were a couple of
aspects that I related to.

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:57.360
I was not formally adopted,
when I was very, very young,

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:02.820
I was taken in by a family that
were not my biological parents.

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:06.810
And I also spent a
very significant time

00:29:06.810 --> 00:29:09.870
in my formative years
going to all-white schools

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:13.560
in all-white neighborhoods.

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:16.400
And so really, when
you started talking

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:21.080
about not seeing yourself
reflected back to yourself,

00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:25.210
I get it 6000%.

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:30.700
But as it relates to
the making of work,

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:37.730
as I'm sitting here
listening to your narrative,

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:44.210
it occurred to me that what
happened with me is that

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:51.350
the aspects that I felt as
a young person and the pain,

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:56.420
deep-seated pain that was
visited on me as a result

00:29:56.420 --> 00:30:00.700
of being a Black kid in
an all-white neighborhood,

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:08.840
a Black kid in all-white schools
and all that that comes with,

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:15.650
I have been able to use in
my work the individual--

00:30:15.650 --> 00:30:20.330
the aloneness, the individual--

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:24.500
what's the word-- the
fact of feeling like one

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:29.690
is alone in the
world I have been

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:36.230
able to use really
constructively,

00:30:36.230 --> 00:30:40.580
not without neuroses,
not without pain

00:30:40.580 --> 00:30:41.780
and all the rest of that.

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:46.740
But in the final analysis, one
of the foundational aspects

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:49.610
of my career,
frankly, is the fact

00:30:49.610 --> 00:30:55.630
that I have been able
to mine those things

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:57.395
for a creative good.

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:04.250
So I'm not quite sure
of what I'm saying other

00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:10.430
than hearing you tell
your story, Gina, A,

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:15.380
inspired me, B, caused
me to reflect on myself.

00:31:15.380 --> 00:31:18.980
And these are not reflections
I haven't had before,

00:31:18.980 --> 00:31:22.940
but just seeing them in a
slightly different light.

00:31:22.940 --> 00:31:26.420
And being able now
all these years later

00:31:26.420 --> 00:31:36.530
to acknowledge, embrace how
the pain of my childhood,

00:31:36.530 --> 00:31:40.310
I've been able to
use and utilize

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:48.950
as an adult. Like big
time, frankly, big time.

00:31:48.950 --> 00:31:51.490
Is there a specific
role, Delroy,

00:31:51.490 --> 00:31:54.320
that you were able to
really use that the most?

00:31:54.320 --> 00:31:56.960
And continue using
it-- continue using it.

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:03.020
The other thing
I want to, I need

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:09.610
to add in the moment
is the fact I mentioned

00:32:09.610 --> 00:32:11.370
at the beginning
of the conversation

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:19.090
that I feel extremely fortunate
that I am working, and I do.

00:32:19.090 --> 00:32:21.190
And this goes, Shawn,
to your question

00:32:21.190 --> 00:32:24.280
of how are you feeling?

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:28.270
Added to the fact
that I am working,

00:32:28.270 --> 00:32:33.830
I'm working on a project
and the last project

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:35.770
that I did with Spike--

00:32:40.030 --> 00:32:46.590
Spike's project and the project
that I'm working on currently

00:32:46.590 --> 00:32:54.090
are both narratives in which
we, as African-descended people,

00:32:54.090 --> 00:32:56.810
are front and center.

00:32:56.810 --> 00:33:03.470
So the narrative is being
told very, very, very much

00:33:03.470 --> 00:33:07.460
from our point of view.

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:14.210
And that enhances the
extent to which I feel--

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:18.220
and I'm sorry I'm going to use
this hackneyed and overused

00:33:18.220 --> 00:33:21.130
word, but blessed because I am.

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:28.850
And the fact that these
projects are not--

00:33:28.850 --> 00:33:30.470
we're in the middle
of-- we've just

00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:33.350
started working on this film
that I'm doing right now,

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:35.510
so I don't know what the
finished result will be.

00:33:35.510 --> 00:33:40.340
But what I can say
is that working

00:33:40.340 --> 00:33:44.270
in the process of doing
this current work,

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:48.290
and absolutely working
in the process of having

00:33:48.290 --> 00:33:58.650
made 5 Bloods, and the fact that
we were front and center is--

00:33:58.650 --> 00:34:03.980
again, another hackneyed and
overused word-- but a gift.

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:11.610
That particularly now,
not only in the America

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:14.400
that we're living in, but the
world that we're living in,

00:34:14.400 --> 00:34:22.139
the fact that I get to be a
part of these projects that

00:34:22.139 --> 00:34:25.590
have nothing to do with
filtering through somebody

00:34:25.590 --> 00:34:29.460
else's gaze or filtering through
somebody else's experience what

00:34:29.460 --> 00:34:33.270
the story is, that's
an extraordinary thing.

00:34:33.270 --> 00:34:37.560
And it does make, for
me, as a creative person,

00:34:37.560 --> 00:34:39.300
as a human being
on the planet, it

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:45.690
makes a very profound and
positive difference to how

00:34:45.690 --> 00:34:54.389
I negotiate my world with all
of its ugliness and the fact

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:58.530
that we, as African-descended
people, are being battered.

00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:03.720
We are so much being
battered right now.

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:08.160
But as a human being, as a
human, as a creative person,

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:14.550
I have these islands,
these oases of affirmations

00:35:14.550 --> 00:35:19.140
for who I am as an
African-descended person,

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:23.740
as a creative person, that
not only can I experience,

00:35:23.740 --> 00:35:26.910
but then I can share with
the rest of the world, which

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:29.140
is extraordinary.

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:34.820
And it is said that in the
specific is the universal,

00:35:34.820 --> 00:35:37.530
and you used the term,
the word universal earlier

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:39.120
in the conversation.

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:42.870
And I get to be a part of--

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:45.150
I've gotten the
gift of being a part

00:35:45.150 --> 00:35:49.860
of very specifically
examining something

00:35:49.860 --> 00:35:53.220
through the lens of who I
am as a Black individual,

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:56.940
sharing that with the world,
and hopefully affirming

00:35:56.940 --> 00:36:01.230
the humanity, not the Black
humanity, the humanity

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:05.170
that exists in those journeys.

00:36:05.170 --> 00:36:06.863
And that's extraordinary.

00:36:10.110 --> 00:36:11.805
Beyond extraordinary.

00:36:11.805 --> 00:36:12.930
I don't have the words to--

00:36:17.160 --> 00:36:20.100
I mean, obviously,
there'd be no reason--

00:36:20.100 --> 00:36:23.010
I had no idea about
your upbringing,

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:27.960
and that's so fascinating
to me because there's always

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:31.590
been a reason that I've
gravitated towards your work.

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:36.660
And in everything,
there is-- and I

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:39.570
know you just said humanity,
not Black humanity.

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:43.320
But for me, in every
role that you played,

00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:46.500
you've always for me
represented Black manhood.

00:36:46.500 --> 00:36:51.150
And it's not just
dignity and strength,

00:36:51.150 --> 00:36:55.470
but this innate vulnerability
that might be from the way

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:57.720
that you grew up and the
things that you talked about.

00:36:57.720 --> 00:37:00.060
But given that all
of those things

00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:05.542
made a Black manhood for
me as opposed to so many

00:37:05.542 --> 00:37:06.750
stereotypes that we're given.

00:37:06.750 --> 00:37:09.330
And so I'm grateful
to you for that.

00:37:09.330 --> 00:37:12.150
And you know I would say
congrats on Da 5 Bloods,

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:14.430
but it's really your
whole body of work

00:37:14.430 --> 00:37:17.940
has always been
that for me, so it's

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:20.700
fascinating to hear where
some of that has come from.

00:37:20.700 --> 00:37:21.510
Thank you.

00:37:21.510 --> 00:37:23.310
We're talking a lot about
owning your stories, which

00:37:23.310 --> 00:37:25.518
is what I captured from what
you were saying, Delroy,

00:37:25.518 --> 00:37:28.932
is as being in the forefront
and behind the scenes narrating,

00:37:28.932 --> 00:37:30.390
and showing and
sharing our stories

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:33.810
and how important it is to have
that authentic through line,

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:36.200
as well as documenting
our stories.

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:39.090
TJ, You've documented
LA 92, which

00:37:39.090 --> 00:37:43.860
has been a story and a
staple in our community.

00:37:43.860 --> 00:37:47.520
Talk to me about LA 92, and
what you've learned about that,

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:50.220
and where you think
we are right now,

00:37:50.220 --> 00:37:52.180
and if there is
any change at all.

00:37:54.750 --> 00:38:00.318
Yeah, for those who
haven't seen LA 92,

00:38:00.318 --> 00:38:05.340
it's a film about the
civil unrest commonly known

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:06.870
as kind of the
Rodney King riots,

00:38:06.870 --> 00:38:10.050
but I prefer civil unrest.

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:15.060
In '92, after Rodney King
was beaten by four motorists

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:18.865
and nearly murdered and then--
sorry, beaten by four cops

00:38:18.865 --> 00:38:22.500
and nearly murdered,
and then the cops

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:23.640
getting off on the trial.

00:38:23.640 --> 00:38:25.990
And then, it
became, to this day,

00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:31.560
the most violent,
monetarily speaking,

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:36.500
the most violent civil
unrest in American history.

00:38:41.102 --> 00:38:45.830
The project was
made in preparation

00:38:45.830 --> 00:38:48.650
for the 25-year
anniversary of the unrest.

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:50.570
And we knew that
there was going to be

00:38:50.570 --> 00:38:52.970
a number of films coming out.

00:38:52.970 --> 00:38:54.485
We did not conceive
of the project.

00:38:54.485 --> 00:38:57.110
It was a production company that
came up with the idea, sold it

00:38:57.110 --> 00:39:01.370
to a network, and they
wanted us to direct it,

00:39:01.370 --> 00:39:05.150
and they sent over a sizzle.

00:39:05.150 --> 00:39:10.600
And in the sizzle, they'd
mined some other early archive.

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:16.490
And there was a moment in
the sizzle where there's

00:39:16.490 --> 00:39:20.720
a gentleman from
South Central that

00:39:20.720 --> 00:39:23.990
was captured on film
within his own community.

00:39:23.990 --> 00:39:29.180
And he's waving a
hammer around, and he's

00:39:29.180 --> 00:39:35.270
yelling at the top of his lungs,
and he's very, very emotional,

00:39:35.270 --> 00:39:37.040
and he's crying.

00:39:37.040 --> 00:39:38.840
He had just been looted.

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:43.070
And he's basically yelling
around within his community,

00:39:43.070 --> 00:39:44.720
why steal from me?

00:39:44.720 --> 00:39:45.980
I come from the ghetto too.

00:39:45.980 --> 00:39:48.830
You call this activism.

00:39:48.830 --> 00:39:52.460
You see a man falling
apart on the camera.

00:39:52.460 --> 00:40:01.990
And for us, the vulnerability
in that particular moment

00:40:01.990 --> 00:40:07.360
was the impetus of
the approach, which

00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:12.610
is can you tell these
events only using archive,

00:40:12.610 --> 00:40:15.160
and preserve the
integrity of the emotion

00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:18.530
that people experienced
in that moment in time?

00:40:18.530 --> 00:40:21.430
So can you create
an experiential film

00:40:21.430 --> 00:40:23.920
and not one that's a
talking head documentary?

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:28.180
I find that,
oftentimes, when we're

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:31.990
looking at critical
moments in our history,

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:34.300
because it's happened
and because there's

00:40:34.300 --> 00:40:40.210
a person giving analysis,
it creates a buffer

00:40:40.210 --> 00:40:42.940
between the audience
and the experience

00:40:42.940 --> 00:40:47.570
that fundamentally
changed people's lives.

00:40:47.570 --> 00:40:48.970
This is a long
way of just saying

00:40:48.970 --> 00:40:54.470
the film is a feature doc only
constructed out of archive.

00:40:54.470 --> 00:41:00.850
And the idea is to create
an immersive experience

00:41:00.850 --> 00:41:03.400
for audiences that may
have lived through it

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:07.270
or for younger audiences,
for younger generations that

00:41:07.270 --> 00:41:09.340
don't know anything about it.

00:41:09.340 --> 00:41:16.360
And we start the film
with a prologue of Watts.

00:41:16.360 --> 00:41:18.220
And then, at the
end of the film,

00:41:18.220 --> 00:41:20.050
it gets pretty
editorialized, where

00:41:20.050 --> 00:41:22.810
we're intercutting
between '65 Watts,

00:41:22.810 --> 00:41:26.850
the civil unrest in Watts,
and the civil unrest in '92.

00:41:26.850 --> 00:41:30.370
And so the dominant
theme of the film

00:41:30.370 --> 00:41:33.710
is that these
events are cyclical.

00:41:33.710 --> 00:41:40.340
It's baked into a
system of violence

00:41:40.340 --> 00:41:45.550
and a system of repression
that will never go away

00:41:45.550 --> 00:41:53.170
until we actually are able to
have people in positions that

00:41:53.170 --> 00:41:56.140
fundamentally hold the
reins of the system

00:41:56.140 --> 00:41:58.130
change the system itself.

00:41:58.130 --> 00:42:03.490
So to answer your question, what
am I seeing differently in 2020

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:10.390
is both on my social
media feed and in Twitter

00:42:10.390 --> 00:42:13.990
and when things really
popped off in LA,

00:42:13.990 --> 00:42:17.950
I was in the middle of
cutting our next film.

00:42:17.950 --> 00:42:20.210
But I just couldn't resist.

00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:24.250
I had to go down and experience
in real time what a protest

00:42:24.250 --> 00:42:27.760
looks like during an epidemic.

00:42:27.760 --> 00:42:30.610
It's a once-in-a-lifetime
opportunity.

00:42:30.610 --> 00:42:32.645
So in all three
instances, what I'm

00:42:32.645 --> 00:42:35.020
seeing on my phone, what I'm
experiencing in person, what

00:42:35.020 --> 00:42:38.930
I'm reading in the news,
the one stand-out thing is

00:42:38.930 --> 00:42:47.390
the diverse coalition of faces
and what seems to be allyship

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:51.500
now, I think, is fundamentally
different than anything

00:42:51.500 --> 00:42:53.370
we've ever seen.

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:57.650
And when I say diverse coalition
I don't mean Black and white.

00:42:57.650 --> 00:43:02.960
I mean Latino, Asian-American,
Native representation,

00:43:02.960 --> 00:43:08.000
all speaking out against
and for and on behalf

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:12.530
of the Black community,
actually showing true allyship

00:43:12.530 --> 00:43:13.880
and empathy.

00:43:13.880 --> 00:43:18.830
Because it felt like there is a
fundamental understanding that

00:43:18.830 --> 00:43:24.050
when one member of our community
is attacked that we all suffer

00:43:24.050 --> 00:43:30.060
as a result. And then we can
lift other communities up,

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:32.000
we are all going to
also benefit from it.

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:35.410
It's really simple,
and it's really clean.

00:43:35.410 --> 00:43:39.730
However, having said that, that
is a fundamental difference.

00:43:39.730 --> 00:43:47.080
But then, the part that I'm
a little bit more wary about

00:43:47.080 --> 00:43:52.180
is because we do live in a
day and age where everyone

00:43:52.180 --> 00:43:55.720
is almost a walking brand
through social media,

00:43:55.720 --> 00:44:02.990
whatever it may be, is
Black pain, is that a fad?

00:44:02.990 --> 00:44:10.010
And is it something that you
are speaking to your community

00:44:10.010 --> 00:44:13.490
by showing support because
that is something that

00:44:13.490 --> 00:44:15.590
is what you are supposed to do?

00:44:15.590 --> 00:44:22.600
And then, what happens when
another Black man gets murdered

00:44:22.600 --> 00:44:27.290
on camera, and then another, and
then another, and then another?

00:44:27.290 --> 00:44:30.530
Does your does your
support fade away

00:44:30.530 --> 00:44:35.330
because the dominant community
is no longer talking about it?

00:44:35.330 --> 00:44:38.370
Does your support only
stop because you don't what

00:44:38.370 --> 00:44:41.450
to do outside just
posting something?

00:44:41.450 --> 00:44:42.225
That's right.

00:44:42.225 --> 00:44:42.725
Yes.

00:44:46.220 --> 00:44:51.390
So to answer your
question, there

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:55.150
is a fundamental change in terms
of what the allies look like.

00:44:55.150 --> 00:44:59.620
I think it's too soon to
tell where we're going.

00:44:59.620 --> 00:45:04.410
And as we talked
about earlier today,

00:45:04.410 --> 00:45:07.320
I'm encouraged by,
especially the younger

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:15.820
voices and their actual very
active palpable participation.

00:45:15.820 --> 00:45:20.310
I'm very much discouraged by
the growing blowback that's

00:45:20.310 --> 00:45:24.280
creating even more
of a divisiveness

00:45:24.280 --> 00:45:29.470
and our inability to have a
healthy, nuanced conversation

00:45:29.470 --> 00:45:34.860
from different ideologies that
usually fundamentally want

00:45:34.860 --> 00:45:39.555
the same things and
oftentimes just don't feel

00:45:39.555 --> 00:45:40.680
like they're getting heard.

00:45:45.870 --> 00:45:50.380
So I think the theme of
LA 92 is alive and well.

00:45:50.380 --> 00:45:57.610
I think we're in a cycle,
a very violent cycle, which

00:45:57.610 --> 00:46:01.010
is also just the
history of this country.

00:46:01.010 --> 00:46:04.210
And I think what
happens next is--

00:46:04.210 --> 00:46:07.210
I think only time will tell.

00:46:07.210 --> 00:46:11.920
That's not to say don't
bow out from really trying

00:46:11.920 --> 00:46:16.240
to have empathetic
conversations,

00:46:16.240 --> 00:46:17.845
even with those that
disagree with you

00:46:17.845 --> 00:46:19.720
because that's actually
what needs to happen.

00:46:19.720 --> 00:46:21.010
Yeah.

00:46:21.010 --> 00:46:24.730
We need to understand where
the vitriol and the hate

00:46:24.730 --> 00:46:30.888
comes from for those
that, let's be honest--

00:46:30.888 --> 00:46:32.680
I don't want to
stereotype, but oftentimes,

00:46:32.680 --> 00:46:35.410
for people what feels
like they don't even

00:46:35.410 --> 00:46:37.780
want people that
look like me or that

00:46:37.780 --> 00:46:42.160
are darker than me to even
be in the same community,

00:46:42.160 --> 00:46:44.500
in the same country.

00:46:44.500 --> 00:46:46.600
I think what I hear you
saying is that we need

00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:47.930
healing through conversation.

00:46:47.930 --> 00:46:49.600
And in order to
have healing, there

00:46:49.600 --> 00:46:52.570
has to be a space where
we can speak our truths.

00:46:52.570 --> 00:46:54.565
And those truths
have to be heard.

00:46:54.565 --> 00:46:57.190
It's not about you understanding
or agreeing with those truths.

00:46:57.190 --> 00:46:58.815
It's just about we
need to be heard.

00:46:58.815 --> 00:47:00.190
And when you speak
about where we

00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:02.980
are in the industry in the
landscape of organizations

00:47:02.980 --> 00:47:04.630
and companies and
Black Lives Matter,

00:47:04.630 --> 00:47:07.540
I've never seen so many people
screaming Black Lives Matter

00:47:07.540 --> 00:47:09.148
that are not Black right now.

00:47:09.148 --> 00:47:11.190
And to your point, I'm
like, what does that mean?

00:47:11.190 --> 00:47:13.600
And that brings
me to my question.

00:47:13.600 --> 00:47:15.730
What is the role and
the responsibility

00:47:15.730 --> 00:47:17.710
of an ally in your opinions?

00:47:17.710 --> 00:47:19.540
What does that look like?

00:47:19.540 --> 00:47:22.660
And knowing that allies
can show their advocacy

00:47:22.660 --> 00:47:24.160
in different ways,
like you said TJ.

00:47:24.160 --> 00:47:27.540
They can show and post
online, but is that a fad?

00:47:27.540 --> 00:47:31.270
When we're no longer talking
about hashtag whoever,

00:47:31.270 --> 00:47:32.050
is that gone?

00:47:32.050 --> 00:47:35.400
What is the ideas of
the roles of allies?

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:37.100
Delroy, were you going
to say something?

00:47:37.100 --> 00:47:37.600
Yeah.

00:47:41.480 --> 00:47:45.350
That's the fundamental question.

00:47:45.350 --> 00:47:51.640
What does alliance
truly look like?

00:47:51.640 --> 00:47:59.900
And TJ, when you said
is Black pain a fad,

00:47:59.900 --> 00:48:07.910
I think that is the subject,
the phenomenon, the thing

00:48:07.910 --> 00:48:12.230
that people who
want to be allies

00:48:12.230 --> 00:48:18.770
have to answer for themselves
so that it is not a fad.

00:48:18.770 --> 00:48:24.860
And that brings me to a short
passage that I wanted to read,

00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:29.700
I want to share, that
I just read recently.

00:48:29.700 --> 00:48:34.590
You all live in Los
Angeles, is that true?

00:48:34.590 --> 00:48:36.040
Mm-hmm, yeah.

00:48:36.040 --> 00:48:36.750
Yeah.

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:37.250
OK.

00:48:40.220 --> 00:48:43.147
The only reason I ask
that because I do not

00:48:43.147 --> 00:48:43.980
live in Los Angeles.

00:48:43.980 --> 00:48:45.313
And that doesn't make me better.

00:48:45.313 --> 00:48:48.140
Just geographically,
it's connected

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:50.550
to what I'm about to read.

00:48:50.550 --> 00:48:54.500
So the question-- and I'm
sorry if I sound like a college

00:48:54.500 --> 00:48:56.180
lecturer right now--

00:48:56.180 --> 00:49:01.490
but I want to ask, does
everybody on this call

00:49:01.490 --> 00:49:05.200
know who Dijon Kizzee is?

00:49:05.200 --> 00:49:07.040
It's Dijon Kizzee.

00:49:07.040 --> 00:49:09.171
I hope I'm pronouncing
his name correctly.

00:49:09.171 --> 00:49:11.930
No clue.

00:49:11.930 --> 00:49:16.610
I didn't know who he who
he was either until I

00:49:16.610 --> 00:49:18.200
read this article.

00:49:18.200 --> 00:49:20.540
So just bear with me one second.

00:49:23.120 --> 00:49:28.446
This is from the Sunday New
York Times of September 13, OK?

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:35.240
"In late August, Los
Angeles Sheriff's deputies

00:49:35.240 --> 00:49:37.030
shot a Black man.

00:49:37.030 --> 00:49:41.090
The Black man's name
is Dijon Kizzee,

00:49:41.090 --> 00:49:46.400
whom they had stopped for a
suspected traffic violation

00:49:46.400 --> 00:49:47.675
as he rode his bicycle.

00:49:50.670 --> 00:49:53.550
He became the seventh
man killed by deputies

00:49:53.550 --> 00:49:57.430
in Los Angeles since the death
of George Floyd in Minneapolis

00:49:57.430 --> 00:50:00.940
on Memorial Day weekend.

00:50:00.940 --> 00:50:08.770
On the same afternoon, state
legislators in Sacramento

00:50:08.770 --> 00:50:12.790
raced to the end of
their 2020 session.

00:50:12.790 --> 00:50:16.940
The most significant
police reform measure

00:50:16.940 --> 00:50:19.070
heralded in the
days of the Black

00:50:19.070 --> 00:50:24.310
Lives Matter marches that
filled the streets did not even

00:50:24.310 --> 00:50:26.070
come up for a vote."

00:50:31.470 --> 00:50:33.840
That, to me-- and
it's a longer article,

00:50:33.840 --> 00:50:36.870
but just those opening
paragraphs, those opening two

00:50:36.870 --> 00:50:41.160
paragraphs, are
profoundly indicative

00:50:41.160 --> 00:50:49.110
of the potential fadism, as
you say, TJ, of Black pain.

00:50:49.110 --> 00:50:55.060
The fact that none of us on
this call, myself included,

00:50:55.060 --> 00:50:59.820
knew who Dijon Kizzee was--

00:50:59.820 --> 00:51:01.990
is.

00:51:01.990 --> 00:51:04.930
He stopped for a suspected
traffic violation

00:51:04.930 --> 00:51:13.280
riding his bicycle and
the fact that the state

00:51:13.280 --> 00:51:19.040
legislature clearly
knew nothing about this,

00:51:19.040 --> 00:51:24.200
and it had no relationship
to whatever legislation was

00:51:24.200 --> 00:51:28.700
on the books having to do
with potential police reform,

00:51:28.700 --> 00:51:36.470
for me, that goes right to the
heart of the potential fadism

00:51:36.470 --> 00:51:43.100
of Black pain and how
when things are no longer

00:51:43.100 --> 00:51:48.510
top of mind for people,
it very quickly reverts

00:51:48.510 --> 00:51:50.685
to business as usual.

00:51:54.470 --> 00:52:00.580
So to your question,
Shawn, what does alliance

00:52:00.580 --> 00:52:06.790
look like, I think that
anybody, anybody on the planet--

00:52:06.790 --> 00:52:08.540
not just in America.

00:52:08.540 --> 00:52:13.180
But anybody on the planet
who views them so or wants

00:52:13.180 --> 00:52:19.260
to view themself as an ally of
Black people, people of color,

00:52:19.260 --> 00:52:25.020
I think you have to ask
yourself the question,

00:52:25.020 --> 00:52:33.110
am I in it for the long haul, or
am I in it for the next month,

00:52:33.110 --> 00:52:35.450
or two, or three?

00:52:35.450 --> 00:52:43.710
Because the reality
is, as we know,

00:52:43.710 --> 00:52:46.410
it's much longer than the
next month, two months, three

00:52:46.410 --> 00:52:50.860
months, next year, two
years, three years.

00:52:50.860 --> 00:52:53.680
So I think allies
have to ask themselves

00:52:53.680 --> 00:53:01.710
the question, the very
hard question, am I down?

00:53:01.710 --> 00:53:04.020
How long am I down for?

00:53:04.020 --> 00:53:09.900
To what extent am I willing
to negotiate my own humanity

00:53:09.900 --> 00:53:12.420
and my own human impulses?

00:53:12.420 --> 00:53:14.850
And that's important--
negotiating

00:53:14.850 --> 00:53:17.730
one's own human impulses.

00:53:17.730 --> 00:53:22.830
Because we as humans,
it is inherently human

00:53:22.830 --> 00:53:24.990
that we go on to the next.

00:53:24.990 --> 00:53:26.670
We deal with
whatever, but then we

00:53:26.670 --> 00:53:29.350
go on to the next that's human.

00:53:29.350 --> 00:53:32.400
But I think for anybody who
really wants to be an ally,

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:36.510
I think the question then
becomes to what extent--

00:53:36.510 --> 00:53:38.485
how long am I in this for?

00:53:38.485 --> 00:53:40.360
Black people don't have
a choice because it's

00:53:40.360 --> 00:53:42.790
our everyday reality.

00:53:42.790 --> 00:53:43.450
Yes.

00:53:43.450 --> 00:53:46.270
For anybody who
wants to be an ally,

00:53:46.270 --> 00:53:49.450
I think they have to
challenge themselves

00:53:49.450 --> 00:53:53.410
with the question, how
long am I down for?

00:53:53.410 --> 00:53:55.910
How long am I in this for?

00:53:55.910 --> 00:53:59.970
What am I prepared to do--

00:53:59.970 --> 00:54:01.910
And also, what are they--

00:54:01.910 --> 00:54:06.780
--in the long term,
over the long haul.

00:54:06.780 --> 00:54:07.510
I'm sorry, Gina.

00:54:07.510 --> 00:54:08.550
Gina?

00:54:08.550 --> 00:54:12.720
Just to coast on this, what
are you willing to give up?

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:15.930
I mean, there's this great
quote that I read, which was,

00:54:15.930 --> 00:54:17.430
when you're in a
position of power,

00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:19.470
equity feels like oppression.

00:54:19.470 --> 00:54:24.150
So when folks
start to feel that,

00:54:24.150 --> 00:54:26.890
are they going to be in
there for the long haul,

00:54:26.890 --> 00:54:29.982
or are they going to
suddenly realize, oh,

00:54:29.982 --> 00:54:32.190
I've got to give up something,
and it's not worth it?

00:54:32.190 --> 00:54:37.980
That's where my fear lies.

00:54:37.980 --> 00:54:41.670
I feel like we're
getting to that point.

00:54:41.670 --> 00:54:43.320
And that article
you just read is

00:54:43.320 --> 00:54:45.630
absolutely indicative of that.

00:54:45.630 --> 00:54:48.710
Yep, yep.

00:54:48.710 --> 00:54:49.670
I don't know.

00:54:49.670 --> 00:54:52.280
I think it's a good question
because I find myself wrestling

00:54:52.280 --> 00:54:57.280
with it all the time.

00:54:57.280 --> 00:55:04.930
And on a little bit more
of an existential level,

00:55:04.930 --> 00:55:09.910
that each individual
has to be willing to do

00:55:09.910 --> 00:55:14.110
a lot of hard self-analysis.

00:55:14.110 --> 00:55:17.315
There has to be a
lot of hard truths

00:55:17.315 --> 00:55:18.940
that they have to
face about themselves

00:55:18.940 --> 00:55:22.170
and their relationship
to society.

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:31.180
Funny enough, I mean, it's
nothing new by any means.

00:55:31.180 --> 00:55:35.700
But for some reason,
Gil Scott-Heron's

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:37.450
"The Revolution Will
Not Be Televised" has

00:55:37.450 --> 00:55:40.790
been top of mind for
me lately, very much so

00:55:40.790 --> 00:55:42.290
as I wrestle with
my own involvement

00:55:42.290 --> 00:55:48.400
and what my
participation looks like,

00:55:48.400 --> 00:55:50.120
not just in bettering
our industry.

00:55:50.120 --> 00:55:55.950
But what's my own activism and
participation in the world look

00:55:55.950 --> 00:55:57.150
like?

00:55:57.150 --> 00:56:00.720
But the reason that has
resonance with me is the crux

00:56:00.720 --> 00:56:06.250
of what he's saying is it won't
be televised because everyone

00:56:06.250 --> 00:56:11.200
has to have a revolution within,
which you cannot capture.

00:56:11.200 --> 00:56:18.010
And I feel like the idea of that
is also at the center of what

00:56:18.010 --> 00:56:19.510
allyship looks like.

00:56:19.510 --> 00:56:21.970
You have to keep pushing
yourself and challenging

00:56:21.970 --> 00:56:25.720
yourself to understand
and even get

00:56:25.720 --> 00:56:33.670
to the place of participating
in a protest, where you're

00:56:33.670 --> 00:56:36.220
participating with empathy,
and you're participating

00:56:36.220 --> 00:56:38.620
by opening your ears.

00:56:38.620 --> 00:56:42.368
That's leadership
to me is listening.

00:56:42.368 --> 00:56:44.410
But that takes someone
who is willing to get over

00:56:44.410 --> 00:56:46.070
their own ego, right?

00:56:46.070 --> 00:56:49.600
And that's that personal
revolution that has to happen.

00:56:49.600 --> 00:56:51.130
And to me, that's fundamental.

00:56:51.130 --> 00:56:54.520
Otherwise, that's also how
we continue this cycle.

00:56:54.520 --> 00:57:00.860
And also, get fooled by just
bad nonsense news and nonsense

00:57:00.860 --> 00:57:05.080
ideas that equity is taking
something away from you.

00:57:05.080 --> 00:57:06.070
What the that?

00:57:06.070 --> 00:57:08.230
You know?

00:57:08.230 --> 00:57:10.780
I actually want to add
something really quickly,

00:57:10.780 --> 00:57:15.060
and that is that alliance--

00:57:15.060 --> 00:57:18.410
and I'm perhaps
stating the obvious,

00:57:18.410 --> 00:57:21.590
and this is very
elementary perhaps.

00:57:21.590 --> 00:57:26.140
But alliance, as we know,
can take many forms.

00:57:26.140 --> 00:57:32.110
And for those of
us or those of you

00:57:32.110 --> 00:57:35.470
who are not comfortable with
marching in the streets,

00:57:35.470 --> 00:57:36.460
that's OK.

00:57:36.460 --> 00:57:38.990
I think that that's OK.

00:57:38.990 --> 00:57:45.130
I think that the question when
one starts to examine oneself,

00:57:45.130 --> 00:57:49.240
and as you say, TJ,
challenge oneself to define

00:57:49.240 --> 00:57:55.750
for oneself what that alliance
looks like, the potentially

00:57:55.750 --> 00:58:02.390
beautiful thing, quote, unquote,
is that I can take many forms,

00:58:02.390 --> 00:58:10.780
as long as one is doing
something on an ongoing basis

00:58:10.780 --> 00:58:12.505
to enact change.

00:58:16.210 --> 00:58:23.420
I would say, TJ and
Gina, all of us,

00:58:23.420 --> 00:58:27.420
we may or may not be
marching in the streets,

00:58:27.420 --> 00:58:31.020
but we are engaging--

00:58:34.607 --> 00:58:35.940
I'm not going to say revolution.

00:58:35.940 --> 00:58:38.670
I'm going to say change agents.

00:58:38.670 --> 00:58:45.060
We are manifesting
the notion of becoming

00:58:45.060 --> 00:58:52.080
living change agents
through our work on one very

00:58:52.080 --> 00:58:56.882
fundamental level, each
and every one of us.

00:58:56.882 --> 00:58:58.340
That's not marching
in the streets.

00:58:58.340 --> 00:58:59.770
I mean, maybe some of us march.

00:58:59.770 --> 00:59:00.950
Maybe some of us do not.

00:59:00.950 --> 00:59:03.040
But I don't want to--

00:59:03.040 --> 00:59:05.680
and I'm sure you do not--

00:59:05.680 --> 00:59:09.820
undervalue, underestimate
the power of cinema,

00:59:09.820 --> 00:59:14.000
of communication, and
that through our work,

00:59:14.000 --> 00:59:20.960
our engaging our work, we are
impacting, frankly, the world.

00:59:20.960 --> 00:59:23.420
And I do not believe
that is hyperbole.

00:59:26.230 --> 00:59:29.290
I believe it to be true.

00:59:29.290 --> 00:59:31.420
I think you're
absolutely right, Delroy.

00:59:31.420 --> 00:59:32.820
And I'm sure--

00:59:32.820 --> 00:59:33.470
I'm not sure.

00:59:33.470 --> 00:59:36.400
But I got a lot of questions
around when the whole unrest

00:59:36.400 --> 00:59:40.390
was happening, and people,
allies or wanting to be allies,

00:59:40.390 --> 00:59:41.950
asking how can I participate?

00:59:41.950 --> 00:59:43.102
What can I do?

00:59:43.102 --> 00:59:43.810
What can I learn?

00:59:43.810 --> 00:59:44.650
What can I read?

00:59:44.650 --> 00:59:45.710
What can I watch?

00:59:45.710 --> 00:59:48.310
And it's like, it's not my
history you need to learn.

00:59:48.310 --> 00:59:49.560
It's yours.

00:59:49.560 --> 00:59:50.500
Mmm.

00:59:50.500 --> 00:59:51.000
[LAUGHS]

00:59:51.000 --> 00:59:51.940
Mmm.

00:59:51.940 --> 00:59:53.500
learn about my history.

00:59:53.500 --> 00:59:55.450
I need you to learn
about your history.

00:59:55.450 --> 00:59:58.030
I need you to understand
what your part--

00:59:58.030 --> 00:59:59.150
and not you specifically.

00:59:59.150 --> 01:00:01.810
You weren't around,
but your family's.

01:00:01.810 --> 01:00:04.395
I need you to understand
because with that understanding

01:00:04.395 --> 01:00:06.520
of your own history comes
what you spoke about, TJ,

01:00:06.520 --> 01:00:07.870
which is accountability.

01:00:07.870 --> 01:00:09.670
And then, once you
understand what that is,

01:00:09.670 --> 01:00:12.520
I need you to go educate
your other friends,

01:00:12.520 --> 01:00:14.530
and then I need you
to amplify that.

01:00:14.530 --> 01:00:16.930
And that's the power
that you have as an ally.

01:00:16.930 --> 01:00:19.660
You have the power
to have those spaces

01:00:19.660 --> 01:00:22.240
and have those relationships
that maybe we don't necessarily

01:00:22.240 --> 01:00:22.960
have.

01:00:22.960 --> 01:00:24.245
But it is your job.

01:00:24.245 --> 01:00:25.870
It is your job to
not learn my history.

01:00:25.870 --> 01:00:27.130
It's your job to learn yours.

01:00:27.130 --> 01:00:29.830
And then it's your job to
not only educate everyone

01:00:29.830 --> 01:00:33.070
around you, but to amplify that
message because that message is

01:00:33.070 --> 01:00:34.960
where there is change.

01:00:34.960 --> 01:00:37.180
And I just wanted to
say to you all here,

01:00:37.180 --> 01:00:39.760
thank you all so much for
your participation today.

01:00:39.760 --> 01:00:43.120
And to your point, Delroy, the
entire reason for the Academy

01:00:43.120 --> 01:00:46.017
Dialogues, again, was because
to have these conversations

01:00:46.017 --> 01:00:48.100
centered around filmmaking,
centered around truth,

01:00:48.100 --> 01:00:51.430
centered around race
relations, gender equality,

01:00:51.430 --> 01:00:55.420
and you are all
affecting the world.

01:00:55.420 --> 01:00:58.270
And we are all watching, and we
are all thankful and grateful

01:00:58.270 --> 01:01:01.480
for your contribution,
not only as humans, but as

01:01:01.480 --> 01:01:06.100
filmmakers to this cinema,
Black cinema, universal cinema,

01:01:06.100 --> 01:01:08.247
all cinema because you
guys do all cinema.

01:01:08.247 --> 01:01:10.330
Artists, I want to thank
you all so much on behalf

01:01:10.330 --> 01:01:13.390
of the Academy for joining
this conversation on Academy

01:01:13.390 --> 01:01:14.500
Dialogues.

01:01:14.500 --> 01:01:18.070
Listen, one really,
really quick thing.

01:01:18.070 --> 01:01:21.540
At the beginning
of this session,

01:01:21.540 --> 01:01:26.130
you referred to
myself, TJ, Gina as--

01:01:30.120 --> 01:01:32.340
I'm paraphrasing that
you spoke about the fact

01:01:32.340 --> 01:01:35.872
that we're all at the
forefront of something.

01:01:35.872 --> 01:01:36.580
Did you say that?

01:01:36.580 --> 01:01:38.170
It was something like that.

01:01:38.170 --> 01:01:39.570
I said affecting.

01:01:39.570 --> 01:01:42.390
I want to say I probably
said affecting culture.

01:01:42.390 --> 01:01:45.500
Yes, yes, something
along those lines.

01:01:45.500 --> 01:01:51.120
And when you said that,
I have to tell you that,

01:01:51.120 --> 01:01:56.360
again, internally,
my response was, OK,

01:01:56.360 --> 01:02:01.070
I hope I'm affecting culture,
whatever it was that you said.

01:02:01.070 --> 01:02:02.400
What I'm aware of--

01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:05.766
and now, this is important
as it relates to fadism.

01:02:05.766 --> 01:02:11.320
What I'm aware of is it I am
having a moment in my career.

01:02:11.320 --> 01:02:12.980
And I'm not being coy.

01:02:12.980 --> 01:02:15.320
I'm not trying to
underplay anything.

01:02:15.320 --> 01:02:18.140
I'm having a moment
right now specifically,

01:02:18.140 --> 01:02:20.540
as it relates to Da 5 Bloods.

01:02:20.540 --> 01:02:22.390
And I'm proud of the film.

01:02:22.390 --> 01:02:23.390
I'm proud of the moment.

01:02:23.390 --> 01:02:24.650
I'm embracing the moment.

01:02:24.650 --> 01:02:30.670
But my job-- and
probably for all of us.

01:02:30.670 --> 01:02:36.370
My job is, A, to
acknowledge that moment,

01:02:36.370 --> 01:02:40.180
to embrace the specialness
of that moment,

01:02:40.180 --> 01:02:41.390
and it's beautiful.

01:02:41.390 --> 01:02:42.230
It's been beautiful.

01:02:42.230 --> 01:02:43.300
It's been extraordinary.

01:02:43.300 --> 01:02:50.780
But my job now is
to keep working

01:02:50.780 --> 01:02:56.780
and not get caught up in
whatever that moment has been,

01:02:56.780 --> 01:02:59.300
may become.

01:02:59.300 --> 01:03:01.530
I want to embrace it.

01:03:01.530 --> 01:03:06.570
But the larger
responsibility that I have

01:03:06.570 --> 01:03:13.290
is to keep working, keep being,
and I think for all of us,

01:03:13.290 --> 01:03:19.170
so that one does not
get sucked into fadism.

01:03:19.170 --> 01:03:21.130
So that's all.

01:03:21.130 --> 01:03:22.240
Good.

01:03:22.240 --> 01:03:23.930
100%.

01:03:23.930 --> 01:03:25.380
Yeah, for all of us, right?

01:03:25.380 --> 01:03:29.770
100%, 100% for all of us.

01:03:29.770 --> 01:03:35.626
I mean, just keeping on
is hard enough, right?

01:03:35.626 --> 01:03:38.720
It is, man.

01:03:38.720 --> 01:03:42.020
I mean, for me, it's
the most important thing

01:03:42.020 --> 01:03:46.010
is we won the Oscar
at a really young age.

01:03:46.010 --> 01:03:52.730
And you're listening to the
way with which other people are

01:03:52.730 --> 01:03:54.900
signing your identity.

01:03:54.900 --> 01:03:58.070
And that's a really
slippery slope

01:03:58.070 --> 01:04:00.603
to lose track of your own voice.

01:04:00.603 --> 01:04:03.020
Because it was early in the
development of even discover--

01:04:03.020 --> 01:04:06.080
I'm still trying to figure out
what my voice is, you know?

01:04:06.080 --> 01:04:11.390
And that's the part that's
where the work is, right?

01:04:11.390 --> 01:04:11.950
That's right.

01:04:11.950 --> 01:04:17.660
I want a career, not a
little blip on the timeline--

01:04:17.660 --> 01:04:18.410
That's right.

01:04:18.410 --> 01:04:19.395
Amen, amen.

01:04:19.395 --> 01:04:20.930
--for myself.

01:04:20.930 --> 01:04:22.170
Exactly right.

01:04:22.170 --> 01:04:24.360
That's exactly right.

01:04:24.360 --> 01:04:24.860
Anyhow--

01:04:28.170 --> 01:04:29.600
We using all this.

01:04:29.600 --> 01:04:33.290
So I love that because
you said your voice,

01:04:33.290 --> 01:04:35.905
and knowing that your
voice can change.

01:04:35.905 --> 01:04:36.780
You know what I mean?

01:04:36.780 --> 01:04:37.613
And you can change--

01:04:37.613 --> 01:04:39.710
And should be allowed to change.

01:04:39.710 --> 01:04:40.210
Yes.

01:04:40.210 --> 01:04:43.340
And your voice should be
allowed to change, as opposed

01:04:43.340 --> 01:04:44.960
to being in a box.

01:04:44.960 --> 01:04:47.330
Yeah, your voice should
be allowed to change.

01:04:47.330 --> 01:04:51.230
Yeah, I heard you speak
a little bit about how

01:04:51.230 --> 01:04:53.890
your hesitation towards
the role in Da 5

01:04:53.890 --> 01:04:57.460
Bloods initially because he
was a Trump supporter, right?

01:04:57.460 --> 01:04:58.220
Yeah.

01:04:58.220 --> 01:05:00.900
And how you sat with that for
a little bit and understanding,

01:05:00.900 --> 01:05:02.850
oh, that needs to be told too.

01:05:02.850 --> 01:05:06.230
And so used your voice
to amplify something

01:05:06.230 --> 01:05:07.833
that still needs to be told.

01:05:07.833 --> 01:05:10.250
And that story still needs to
be told because that's still

01:05:10.250 --> 01:05:12.920
clearly the human experience.

01:05:12.920 --> 01:05:15.830
And so your voice can
be allowed to change.

01:05:15.830 --> 01:05:16.820
We're all artists.

01:05:16.820 --> 01:05:19.162
In any sense of the word,
if you're creating anything,

01:05:19.162 --> 01:05:21.620
if you're living in this world,
you are creating something.

01:05:21.620 --> 01:05:22.640
You're an artist.

01:05:22.640 --> 01:05:26.750
And it's your job involved to
reinvent, to create new space,

01:05:26.750 --> 01:05:28.760
to figure out what
the pipeline is,

01:05:28.760 --> 01:05:31.520
to create a space where
you're mentoring and bringing

01:05:31.520 --> 01:05:32.120
in new people.

01:05:32.120 --> 01:05:34.910
But it's your job to
continue finding yourself

01:05:34.910 --> 01:05:36.860
because that is your
job to then reflect it

01:05:36.860 --> 01:05:38.556
back to everybody else.

01:05:38.556 --> 01:05:39.550
Amen.

01:05:39.550 --> 01:05:40.220
So true.

01:05:40.220 --> 01:05:40.720
Yeah.

01:05:40.720 --> 01:05:40.920
So true.

01:05:40.920 --> 01:05:42.710
